R = {
"Portuguese begin Atlantic slave trade": ("EP",3,"Portuguese sailors seized Africans off the Saharan coast, beginning a trade that would move 12.5 million people.","Expeditions sent by Prince Henry the Navigator began capturing and buying Africans on the coast of today's Mauritania and selling them in Portugal. Within decades Portugal was buying captives from African states along the coast.","After 1500 the trade shifted to supplying sugar plantations in the Americas.","The Atlantic slave trade reshaped three continents: Africa's demography, the Americas' populations and Europe's wealth."),
"Sonni Ali builds the Songhai Empire": ("PW",2,"A warrior king built the largest empire in West African history.","Sonni Ali led cavalry and a river fleet on the Niger to conquer Timbuktu and Djenné, the great trading and learning cities of the Mali Empire.","Songhai controlled the trans-Saharan trade in gold and salt for over a century.","Mali and Niger look back on Songhai as part of their national history."),
"Portuguese build Elmina Castle": ("EP",2,"Portugal built the first European fort in sub-Saharan Africa on the Gold Coast.","Elmina was built to control the gold trade with local Akan states. Later it held enslaved Africans awaiting ships to the Americas.","The Dutch took it in 1637; the British later controlled the coast.","Elmina is a UNESCO site; its 'Door of No Return' draws visitors from the African diaspora."),
"Dias rounds the Cape of Good Hope": ("ES",3,"A Portuguese captain proved you could sail around Africa.","Bartolomeu Dias was blown far south by a storm and found he had passed Africa's southern tip. His crew forced him to turn back.","Vasco da Gama followed his route to India ten years later.","It opened the sea route between Europe and Asia that powered globalization."),
"Askia Muhammad: Songhai's peak": ("PRC",2,"Under Askia Muhammad, Songhai reached its height and Timbuktu became a world center of learning.","Askia took power in a coup, organized a professional army and administration, and promoted Islamic scholarship. Timbuktu's mosques and libraries drew scholars from across the Muslim world.","Songhai prospered for a century until Morocco invaded.","Timbuktu's manuscripts, hundreds of thousands, are a treasure of world literature."),
"Portuguese sack Kilwa": ("WE",3,"Portuguese ships looted Kilwa, the jewel of the Swahili coast.","Kilwa Kisiwani, in today's Tanzania, controlled the gold trade from Great Zimbabwe and had minted its own coins for centuries. Francisco de Almeida's fleet stormed and plundered it.","Portugal tried to control Indian Ocean trade by force; the Swahili cities declined.","Kilwa's ruins are a UNESCO site; the Swahili coast's mixed African and Arab culture lives on."),
"Ottomans conquer Egypt": ("PW",2,"The Ottomans swept away the Mamluks and took Egypt.","Selim I defeated the Mamluk sultanate, gaining Egypt, Syria and control of Mecca and Medina.","Egypt stayed under Ottoman rule for nearly 300 years.","Ottoman control of Islam's holy cities made the sultan the leading Muslim ruler."),
"Kongo's King Afonso protests slave trade": ("PRE",2,"A Christian African king wrote to Portugal begging it to stop kidnapping his people.","King Afonso I of Kongo had converted to Christianity and allied with Portugal. But Portuguese traders were seizing Kongo's people, even nobles. He wrote to King João III to stop it.","Portugal ignored him; the slave trade grew and helped destabilize Kongo.","His letters are among the earliest African written protests against the slave trade."),
"Adal invades Ethiopia": ("WR",2,"A Muslim general nearly conquered Christian Ethiopia.","Imam Ahmad of Adal, using Ottoman muskets, overran most of the Ethiopian highlands, burning churches.","Portuguese musketeers helped Ethiopia defeat him in 1543.","Ethiopia survived as one of the world's oldest Christian kingdoms."),
"Morocco destroys Songhai": ("W",2,"Moroccan musketeers crossed the Sahara and destroyed Songhai.","Morocco's sultan wanted Songhai's gold. His army, with firearms, beat Songhai's cavalry at Tondibi.","West Africa's last great empire broke apart; trade shifted to the Atlantic coast.","It's a reminder that gunpowder empires reshaped Africa too."),
"Dutch found Cape Town": ("PE",3,"The Dutch East India Company built a supply station at the Cape.","Jan van Riebeeck set up a fort and vegetable gardens to supply Dutch ships sailing to Asia. Settlers soon pushed into Khoikhoi lands and imported enslaved people from Asia and Africa.","Dutch settlers became the Afrikaners.","South Africa's racial divisions trace back to this colony."),
"Omanis take Mombasa": ("WP",2,"Omani Arabs captured Fort Jesus, ending Portuguese power on the Swahili coast.","After a siege of nearly three years, the Omanis took Mombasa's Portuguese fort.","Oman controlled the coast and its trade for 150 years.","Oman's influence shaped Swahili culture, architecture and Zanzibar's later rise."),
"Asante Empire rises": ("P",2,"The Asante united around the Golden Stool and became West Africa's great power.","Osei Tutu and priest Okomfo Anokye united Akan states; legend says the Golden Stool descended from the sky to hold the nation's soul.","Asante grew rich on gold and trade and fought the British into 1900.","The Asante king still holds great cultural authority in Ghana."),
"Britain takes the Cape": ("PW",2,"Britain seized the Cape Colony from the Dutch.","During the wars with Napoleon, Britain took the Cape to protect its route to India.","British rule and its abolition of slavery angered Dutch settlers.","It led to the Great Trek and the Boer Wars."),
"Sokoto Caliphate founded": ("RP",2,"A scholar's holy war built West Africa's largest state.","Usman dan Fodio, a Fulani Islamic teacher, led a jihad against Hausa kings he saw as corrupt.","The caliphate ruled northern Nigeria until the British conquered it in 1903.","The Sultan of Sokoto is still a key religious leader in Nigeria."),
"Britain bans slave trade": ("PE",3,"Britain outlawed the slave trade and began patrolling West Africa.","The Royal Navy's West Africa Squadron intercepted slave ships.","Freed Africans were often resettled in Sierra Leone.","It began the end of the Atlantic trade."),
"Shaka builds the Zulu kingdom": ("PW",2,"Shaka forged the Zulu into southern Africa's most powerful kingdom.","Shaka reformed warfare with short stabbing spears and disciplined regiments, and absorbed rival clans.","The upheaval, the Mfecane, pushed peoples across southern Africa.","Shaka is a hero of Zulu identity."),
"Liberia founded": ("P",2,"Freed Black Americans founded Liberia in West Africa.","The American Colonization Society, mixing abolitionists and slaveholders who wanted free Black people out of the US, sent settlers.","Liberia became Africa's first republic in 1847, ruled by Americo-Liberians.","Tensions between settlers' descendants and indigenous Liberians fueled civil wars."),
"France invades Algeria": ("WP",3,"France invaded Algeria and began 132 years of colonial rule.","Using a diplomatic insult as a pretext, France captured Algiers and conquered the country over decades.","Hundreds of thousands of European settlers moved in.","Algeria's 1954–62 war of independence still shapes French-Algerian relations."),
"Boer Great Trek": ("P",2,"Dutch-speaking settlers trekked inland to escape British rule.","Thousands of Boers left the Cape with wagons, angry at British abolition of slavery.","They founded the Transvaal and Orange Free State.","It's central to Afrikaner nationalism and the roots of apartheid."),
"Oman moves capital to Zanzibar": ("PE",2,"Oman's sultan moved his capital to Zanzibar, making it East Africa's trading hub.","Sultan Said bin Sultan built clove plantations worked by enslaved Africans.","Zanzibar became the center of the East African ivory and slave trade.","Zanzibar's Stone Town is a UNESCO site of Swahili-Arab culture."),
"Suez Canal opens": ("ES",3,"The Suez Canal linked the Mediterranean and Red Sea.","Built by Ferdinand de Lesseps with forced Egyptian labor, it cut the voyage from Europe to India by thousands of miles.","Egypt went into debt and Britain occupied it.","About 12% of world trade passes through it."),
"Zanzibar slave market closed": ("PE",2,"Britain forced Zanzibar to close East Africa's biggest slave market.","Under British naval threat, Sultan Barghash shut the market.","Slavery itself was abolished in Zanzibar only in 1897.","An Anglican cathedral now stands on the site."),
"Anglo-Zulu War": ("W",2,"The Zulu crushed a British army at Isandlwana, then lost the war.","Britain invaded Zululand to remove an independent power. The Zulu won at Isandlwana, killing over 1,300 British troops.","Britain returned with more troops and destroyed the Zulu kingdom.","Isandlwana is one of the greatest defeats of a colonial army by an African force."),
"Britain occupies Egypt": ("PWE",3,"Britain took over Egypt to control the Suez Canal.","After Egypt went bankrupt and a nationalist revolt broke out, British forces occupied the country.","Britain controlled Egypt until the 1950s.","It began the Scramble for Africa."),
"Berlin Conference: Scramble for Africa": ("PE",3,"European powers met in Berlin to divide Africa, with no Africans present.","Bismarck hosted 14 nations; they agreed claims needed 'effective occupation', triggering a land grab.","By 1914, Europe controlled about 90% of Africa.","Many borders drawn then still cause conflicts."),
"Congo Free State; German East Africa": ("PE",3,"King Leopold took Congo as his private property, and Germany claimed East Africa.","Leopold's agents forced Congolese to collect rubber, cutting off hands for failing quotas. Germany claimed Tanganyika, Rwanda and Burundi.","Millions died in Leopold's Congo.","It's one of colonialism's worst atrocities."),
"Ethiopia defeats Italy at Adwa": ("WP",3,"Ethiopia crushed an Italian invasion and stayed independent.","Emperor Menelik II, with modern rifles, defeated Italy's army at Adwa.","Ethiopia was the only African state to defeat a European power and stay free.","Adwa inspired pan-Africanism; Ethiopia's colors appear on many African flags."),
"Anglo-Zanzibar War": ("W",1,"Britain bombarded Zanzibar in the shortest war in history.","When a new sultan took power without British approval, British ships shelled his palace for about 40 minutes.","A pro-British sultan was installed.","It's the shortest recorded war."),
"Boer War": ("W",3,"Britain fought the Boer republics for gold, using concentration camps.","Britain wanted the Transvaal's gold fields. Boer guerrillas fought on; Britain burned farms and held about 100,000 civilians in camps where tens of thousands died.","Britain won and created the Union of South Africa.","It was a dark precursor of 20th-century warfare."),
"Herero & Nama genocide": ("W",3,"Germany carried out the first genocide of the 20th century in Namibia.","After the Herero and Nama rose against land seizures, General von Trotha ordered their extermination. Survivors were driven into the desert or concentration camps.","Up to 80% of the Herero died.","Germany acknowledged it as genocide in 2021."),
"Maji Maji Rebellion": ("WR",3,"Southern Tanganyika rose against German rule, armed with belief in sacred water.","Forced to grow cotton for export, communities united behind the prophet Kinjikitile, whose 'maji' (water) was said to turn bullets to water.","Germany crushed it with a scorched-earth campaign; up to 300,000 died, mostly from famine.","Tanzania honors it as a founding moment of national unity."),
"Belgium takes Congo from Leopold": ("P",2,"International outrage forced Leopold to give up Congo.","Journalists and activists exposed atrocities; Belgium annexed the colony.","Belgian rule continued until 1960.","It was one of the first modern human-rights campaigns."),
"Union of South Africa": ("P",2,"South Africa became a self-governing country, ruled by whites.","Britain joined its colonies with the Boer republics into one dominion.","The ANC was founded in 1912 to fight for Black rights.","It set up decades of white-minority rule."),
"WWI East Africa campaign": ("W",2,"World War I raged across East Africa for four years.","German commander Lettow-Vorbeck led British forces on a long chase across Tanzania, Kenya and Mozambique.","Hundreds of thousands of African porters and civilians died.","It's a forgotten front of WWI."),
"Egypt nominally independent": ("P",2,"Britain declared Egypt independent but kept control.","After the 1919 revolution, Britain granted independence while keeping troops and the canal.","Nasser's 1952 revolution ended British influence.","Egypt became the leading voice of Arab nationalism."),
"Italy invades Ethiopia": ("WP",3,"Mussolini conquered Ethiopia with poison gas.","Italy invaded to avenge Adwa. Haile Selassie appealed to the League of Nations, which failed to act.","Ethiopia was freed in 1941.","The League's failure encouraged Hitler."),
"Apartheid begins": ("P",3,"South Africa made racial segregation the law of the land.","The National Party won and passed laws classifying everyone by race and separating them in housing, jobs and marriage.","Resistance and international pressure grew for decades.","Apartheid ended in 1994; its economic inequality remains."),
"Mau Mau uprising": ("WP",3,"Kenyan fighters rose against British rule, and Britain built camps.","Kikuyu fighters demanded land taken by white settlers. Britain detained about 150,000 people, with widespread torture.","Kenya won independence in 1963.","Britain paid compensation to survivors in 2013."),
"Algerian War": ("WP",3,"Algeria fought a brutal war to end French rule.","The FLN launched an uprising. France used torture and mass detention; about a million settlers left after independence.","Algeria won independence in 1962.","It still shapes French politics and memory."),
"Suez Crisis": ("PW",3,"Egypt nationalized the Suez Canal and humiliated Britain and France.","Britain, France and Israel invaded; the US forced them out.","Nasser became a hero; Britain's imperial power was broken.","It's a turning point for decolonization."),
"Ghana independent": ("P",3,"Ghana became the first sub-Saharan colony to win independence.","Kwame Nkrumah's mass movement won independence from Britain.","Nkrumah championed pan-Africanism and inspired independence across the continent.","Ghana's independence started the decolonization of Africa."),
"'Year of Africa'": ("P",3,"Seventeen African nations won independence in one year.","Nigeria, Congo, Senegal and others became free.","New states faced Cold War pressures and fragile borders.","Most of Africa's modern map dates from this decade."),
"Lumumba killed; Tanganyika independent": ("PW",3,"Congo's elected leader was murdered, while Tanganyika won independence peacefully.","Lumumba was overthrown and killed with Belgian and US involvement. Nyerere led Tanganyika to independence.","Congo descended into crisis; Nyerere became a statesman.","Both shaped Africa's post-colonial path."),
"OAU founded; Kenya independent": ("P",3,"Africa founded a continental union, and Kenya became independent.","The OAU was founded in Addis Ababa; Kenya won independence under Jomo Kenyatta.","The OAU backed liberation movements across southern Africa.","It became the African Union in 2002."),
"Tanzania formed": ("P",2,"Tanganyika and Zanzibar merged into Tanzania.","After a violent revolution in Zanzibar, Nyerere and Karume united the two.","Tanzania became a stable, non-aligned state.","Zanzibar keeps its own semi-autonomous government."),
"Biafran War; Arusha Declaration": ("WPI",3,"Nigeria fought a civil war, while Tanzania launched African socialism.","Biafra tried to secede; Nigeria blockaded it, causing famine. Nyerere's Arusha Declaration launched Ujamaa, collective villages and self-reliance.","About 1–2 million died in Biafra; Ujamaa reshaped Tanzania.","Both remain touchstones in African politics."),
"Haile Selassie overthrown": ("P",2,"Ethiopia's emperor was deposed after 44 years.","A Marxist military junta, the Derg, took power during famine and unrest.","Mengistu's 'Red Terror' killed tens of thousands.","Ethiopia's monarchy ended."),
"Angola & Mozambique independent": ("PW",2,"Portugal's African empire collapsed.","After Portugal's own revolution, Angola and Mozambique became independent.","Both fell into Cold War civil wars.","Angola's war lasted until 2002."),
"Tanzania ousts Idi Amin": ("WP",3,"Tanzania invaded Uganda and toppled Idi Amin.","After Amin invaded Tanzania, Nyerere's army counterattacked and took Kampala.","Amin fled; Uganda remained unstable.","It's one of Africa's rare interventions against a dictator."),
"Zimbabwe independent": ("P",2,"Zimbabwe won majority rule after a bush war.","Robert Mugabe won elections after Rhodesia's white government gave up.","Mugabe ruled until 2017, with economic collapse.","It ended white minority rule in Rhodesia."),
"Ethiopian famine": ("PE",2,"Famine killed up to a million Ethiopians, and the world watched.","Drought and civil war caused famine. Live Aid raised money worldwide.","It reshaped how the West thinks about aid.","Famine remains a risk in the Horn of Africa."),
"Mandela freed": ("P",3,"Nelson Mandela walked free after 27 years in prison.","Under pressure from sanctions and protests, de Klerk released Mandela and legalized the ANC.","Negotiations led to democratic elections in 1994.","Mandela became a global symbol of reconciliation."),
"Rwandan genocide; Mandela president": ("WP",3,"Rwanda's genocide killed 800,000 in 100 days, while South Africa held its first free election.","Hutu extremists slaughtered Tutsi and moderate Hutu. Mandela became South Africa's first Black president.","Rwanda's genocide spilled into Congo's wars.","It led to the 'Responsibility to Protect' doctrine."),
"Congo wars begin": ("W",3,"The Congo wars drew in nine countries and killed millions.","Rwanda and Uganda invaded to chase genocide perpetrators and seize minerals.","Millions died, mostly from disease and hunger.","Conflict continues in eastern Congo."),
"African Union founded": ("P",2,"The African Union replaced the OAU.","African leaders created a body with peacekeeping powers.","It deployed forces in Somalia and elsewhere.","The AU joined the G20 in 2023."),
"Darfur": ("W",3,"Sudan's government and militias killed hundreds of thousands in Darfur.","Janjaweed militias attacked non-Arab villages.","The ICC charged Sudan's president with genocide.","Darfur is again a battlefield in Sudan's current war."),
"Arab Spring; South Sudan independent": ("PW",3,"Revolutions toppled North African dictators, and South Sudan became a nation.","Protests toppled Ben Ali, Mubarak and Gaddafi. South Sudan voted to secede after decades of war.","Libya fell into chaos; South Sudan into civil war.","Tunisia's democracy later backslid."),
"Ebola outbreak": ("S",2,"Ebola killed about 11,000 in West Africa.","The virus spread through Guinea, Liberia and Sierra Leone.","Global response improved epidemic preparedness.","An Ebola vaccine was approved in 2019."),
"Tigray war": ("W",2,"Ethiopia's Tigray war killed hundreds of thousands.","Fighting broke out between Ethiopia's government and the TPLF.","A peace deal was signed in 2022.","It damaged Ethiopia's reputation."),
"Sahel coups": ("PW",2,"Military coups swept the Sahel.","Juntas took power in Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger.","France was pushed out; Russia moved in.","The region remains unstable."),
"Sudan civil war": ("W",3,"Sudan's generals went to war, causing the world's largest displacement crisis.","The army and RSF fought over power.","Millions were displaced; famine spread.","It's one of the world's worst humanitarian crises."),
}
